Z||000000||Ironhorse||08-08-2000||03:33 PM||||Hello to you all.  For 4 months I've been working my ass off getting my 85 GT motor put together, putting new sensors, plugs, and etc.  When I put it in several weeks ago, it wouldn't fire up.  I took it down to my friend that own the garage so that he can hook it up to the computer.  I had the throttle position sensor in wrong, and now it fire right up.  I asked him if everything works ok.. he said his computer told him that everything works in perfect shape.  I brought it home.  I tried to time it, couldn't found that 4 out of 6 spark plugs wires was bad.  I bought a Accel spark plug wires and slap it on.  It's doing the same thing, along with that it idles at 2500rpm and stay there, when shift into gear it drop to 1500 rpm, and after about 10 seconds of running, it would die no matter what I do.  I've done about everything that I could do and replace just about everything in that friggin car and I just can't get it to run, and idle enough.  I know all about driving 40 mph and etc to set the computer but I coudln't get it running long enough to do that.  WHAT'S NEXT1?!?!?!?!?!?!?!?!?!?!?!?!?!?!?!?!?!?!?!? I've put 1800 dollars into it and no result!   Thanks<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||08-08-2000||03:55 PM||||look at the rest of the ignition parts. cap, rotor pickup coil and PIM. make sure the PIM is installed with "heatsink" grease. You can get that at radio shack. It's better that "dielectric" which is all the car parts store will have.||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||08-08-2000||03:58 PM||||"Where there's smoke, there's work" Boil M. Owl of the Bowel Oil Company<P>Translated it means that you have to put up with some frustration to reach the goal. Hang in there and work through each one of the problems. <P>It sounds like you have a vacuum leak. You can use a water mister or carb cleaner (very obvious when it is sucked in and burnt) to check for leaks. <P>First, check that the throttle plate is not binding and allowing air to pass around it. <P>Second, have someone turn the key off and listen for the IAC reseting (park position). If it is moving, odds are it's ok. When the key is turned off, it moves to the park position.<P>Check the EGR tube, the throttle body gasket, the brake booster hose, the canister ports and the EGR vacuum port. <P>Also, check the idle hose that goes from the intake to top plemnum/TB. Clamp all rubber hoses. This hose is famous for spliting and leaking.<P>Spray the water or cleaner and watch or a changing in RPM or a puff from the exhaust. That's your leak.<P>If necessary, start blocking all vacuum ports until you find the one that's leaking. <P>Any air that enters the intake other than through the front of the throttle body and then through the IAC passage and down the hose to the lower intake will make the idle too high. That is air that the IAC is not metering. <P>When you are finished you'll have more knowledge that will keep you from having the problem again.<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 08-08-2000).]||||reg||14
